    "summary": "Scorching Playin' jam, soaring Mountain Jam, tight funk throughout",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blistering \"Playing In The Band\" jam and an epic Mountain Jam encore with guest Allman Brothers. Jerry’s guitar soars while Phil lays down zip‑like bass riffs, and the band rides a deep funk groove on tracks like \"Loose Lucy\" and \"Good Lovin'\" (captured in the best board tape). Overall it’s a high‑energy performance that ranks among the summer ’73 highlights.",
    "review": "Watkins Glen on July 28 1973 is remembered as one of the band’s most electrifying midsummer outings. The first set rolls through classic material with a funk‑driven \"Playing In The Band\" that showcases Jerry’s dynamic tempo shifts and Bob’s gritty rhythm picking, while Phil’s bass runs add excitement at every turn. The second set builds momentum into a blistering \"He's Gone → Truckin'\" segue, a soaring China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, and a beautifully melodic stretch of \"Eyes Of The World\" and \"Stella Blue\". The night culminates in an historic encore where the Dead lock horns with the Allman Brothers for a monster Mountain Jam, giving Keith a chance to shine on dual keyboards and Bill a solid, grooving foundation. The crystal‑clear soundboard recording (gd1973-07-28.141306.sbd.eaton.miller.clugston.flac1644) captures every nuance – from the crisp guitar leads to the superb instrument separation – making this show a must‑listen for anyone chasing that 1973 magic.",
